# Which binaries are stored outside the Git objects.
#
# Git keeps every version of a file whole. An 8 MB mesh replaced three times is 24 MB in
# every clone from then on, and the only fix afterwards rewrites history. Git LFS keeps
# those bytes on the server beside the repository and commits a pointer of a couple of
# hundred characters in their place.
#
# What this repository has that needs it: test assets. Nothing here is generated art —
# the plugin's own tests build their inputs in code — so the only binaries that ever
# land are artefacts copied from a real project to be tested against, and those are
# meshes. A clone needs `git lfs install` once per machine before any of it applies.
#
# *.png is deliberately absent. A reference drawing is 10–21 KB, never rewritten, and
# reading one out of a pointer is a step nobody should have to take to run the tests.
#
# *.gltf is absent for the reason Cottony's own list gives: that form leaves its payload
# in a .bin beside it, and *.bin is far too generic an extension to route here. Commit
# the self-contained .glb.
#
# Adding a type: add its line, then `git add --renormalize .` for anything already
# committed under it, because a pattern on its own converts nothing already in history.

# 3D meshes, which arrive from the generative service at tens of MB and are the whole
# reason this file exists.
*.glb       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.fbx       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.blend     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Captures and recordings, where a test ever needs one of either.
*.mp4       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mov       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.webm      filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ogg       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
